IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `crtpa-kleene`
IMPORT `crtpa-nouns`

-- Contracts (Rights of Third Parties) Act 2001 (Singapore), 2020 Revised Edition, informal
-- consolidation -- LAYER 1, SECTION 8: SUPPLEMENTARY PROVISIONS RELATING TO THIRD PARTY.
--
-- What is encoded: s 8(1) (section 2 does not take away other rights), (2) (the Unfair Contract Terms Act 1977 s 2(2)
-- is switched off for a third party's claim), (3) (the Limitation Act 1959 s 6 reaches a third party's action) and (4)
-- (the third party is not made a party to the contract for another written law by three routes).
-- The Unfair Contract Terms Act 1977 and the Limitation Act 1959 are not among the sources: what they provide is asked
-- as a fact, and their periods are inputs.
--
-- READING A RESULT.  JUST TRUE and JUST FALSE are answers the Act itself gives; NOTHING means the Act does not
-- answer on these facts (NOTES.md, "Reading a result: FALSE and NOTHING").

§ `Section 8(1) -- rights and remedies apart from this Act`

-- src:332 | 8.—(1) Section 2 does not affect any right or remedy of a third party
-- src:333 | that exists or is available apart from this Act.
@ref s 8(1) (src:332-333)
GIVEN `the right or remedy exists or is available apart from this Act` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 8(1) section 2 affects a right or remedy of the third party that exists or is available apart from this Act` MEANS
    IF `the right or remedy exists or is available apart from this Act`
    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "Section 2 does not affect any right or remedy of a third party that exists or is available apart from this Act."
    THEN JUST FALSE
    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the negative reaches only a right or remedy "that exists or is available apart from this Act"; one that exists only under this Act is outside it
    ELSE NOTHING

§ `Section 8(2) -- the Unfair Contract Terms Act 1977, section 2(2)`

-- src:335 | (2) Section 2(2) of the Unfair Contract Terms Act 1977 (exclusion
-- src:336 | of or restriction on liability for negligence) does not apply where the
-- src:337 | negligence consists of the breach of an obligation arising from a term
-- src:338 | of a contract and the person seeking to enforce it is a third party acting
-- src:339 | in reliance on section 2.
@ref s 8(2) (src:335-339)
GIVEN `the negligence consists of the breach of an obligation arising from a term of a contract` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the person seeking to enforce the obligation is a third party acting in reliance on section 2` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 8(2) section 2(2) of the Unfair Contract Terms Act 1977 does not apply` MEANS
    IF `the negligence consists of the breach of an obligation arising from a term of a contract`
       AND `the person seeking to enforce the obligation is a third party acting in reliance on section 2`
    -- TRUE: the text's own "does not apply where ..."
    THEN JUST TRUE
    -- FALSE-kind (a failed "where"; the periods-and-where ruling of 2026-11-05, rule (b)): "does not apply where the negligence consists of the breach of an obligation arising from a term of a contract and the person seeking to enforce it is a third party acting in reliance on section 2"; this section switches the Unfair Contract Terms Act 1977 section 2(2) off on no other facts
    ELSE JUST FALSE

§ `Section 8(3) -- the Limitation Act 1959, section 6`

-- src:341 | (3) In section 6 of the Limitation Act 1959, the references to an
-- src:342 | action founded on a contract include references to an action brought
-- src:343 | in reliance on section 2 relating to a contract.
@ref s 8(3) (src:341-343)
GIVEN `the action is brought in reliance on section 2 relating to a contract` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 8(3) the action is one founded on a contract for the purposes of section 6 of the Limitation Act 1959` MEANS
    IF `the action is brought in reliance on section 2 relating to a contract`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    -- NOTHING-kind (includes): "the references to an action founded on a contract include references to an action brought in reliance on section 2"; another action is neither included nor excluded
    ELSE NOTHING

-- The period section 6 of the Limitation Act 1959 gives for an action founded on a contract is not among the sources,
-- so it is an input; the yes/no function above carries the "no".
-- src:341 | (3) In section 6 of the Limitation Act 1959, the references to an
-- src:342 | action founded on a contract include references to an action brought
-- src:343 | in reliance on section 2 relating to a contract.
@ref s 8(3) the period (src:341-343)
GIVEN `the action is brought in reliance on section 2 relating to a contract` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the period section 6 of the Limitation Act 1959 gives for an action founded on a contract, in years` IS A MAYBE NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`s 8(3) the period that applies to the action, in years` MEANS
    IF `the action is brought in reliance on section 2 relating to a contract`
    -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): the period is given by section 6 of the Limitation Act 1959, which is not among the sources, so the person's statement of it is the answer, and NOTHING when not stated
    THEN `the period section 6 of the Limitation Act 1959 gives for an action founded on a contract, in years`
    -- NOTHING-kind (includes): the action is not one the extension names, so the text states no period for it
    ELSE NOTHING

§ `Section 8(4) -- not treated as a party for another written law`

-- src:345 | (4) A third party is not, by virtue of section 2(5) or 4(4) or (6),
-- src:346 | treated as a party to the contract for the purposes of any other written
-- src:347 | law.
@ref s 8(4) (src:345-347)
GIVEN `the route` IS A `Route by which the third party might be treated as a party`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 8(4) the third party is treated as a party to the contract for the purposes of any other written law` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the route`
    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "A third party is not, by virtue of section 2(5) or 4(4) or (6), treated as a party to the contract for the purposes of any other written law."
    WHEN `section 2(5)` THEN JUST FALSE
    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "A third party is not, by virtue of section 2(5) or 4(4) or (6), treated as a party to the contract for the purposes of any other written law."
    WHEN `section 4(4)` THEN JUST FALSE
    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "A third party is not, by virtue of section 2(5) or 4(4) or (6), treated as a party to the contract for the purposes of any other written law."
    WHEN `section 4(6)` THEN JUST FALSE
    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the negative is "by virtue of section 2(5) or 4(4) or (6)"; a third party may be treated as a party by another provision (section 9 does so for arbitration)
    WHEN `a provision other than section 2(5), 4(4) or 4(6)` THEN NOTHING
